# Copy to deploy/.env (gitignored) and fill in.

# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   openssl rand -hex 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ── Access keys ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Named, scoped, hashed. Mint each one with:
#   cd server-portable && bun keygen.ts --name laptop  --scope write
#   cd server-portable && bun keygen.ts --name chatgpt --scope read
#   cd server-portable && bun keygen.ts --name session-hook --scope capture
#
# Store the HASHES here, never the keys — the server only ever compares digests,
# so this value is not a usable credential on its own. Comma- or newline-separated.
#
# Give read scope to anything that only searches, and to any client that needs the
# key in a URL: capture_thought is not registered for a read key, so it cannot
# write even if the URL leaks into logs or browser history. Give capture scope to
# a hook or a pipeline that only adds thoughts (recipes/session-capture-hook):
# capture_thought is the one tool registered for it, so a leak can add, not read.
MCP_ACCESS_KEYS=

# Each key name here becomes an agent in ob1_agents on first use, with a stable
# id that survives both rotating the key (keep the name, change the digest) and
# renaming it (keep the digest, change the name). Every write is attributed to
# that id in thought_audit.canonical_agent_id.
#
# To revoke a key WITHOUT editing this file and restarting, take its digest from
# above and run against the database:
#
#   SELECT revoke_agent_key('<digest>', 'why');
#
# It takes effect within OB1_AGENT_CACHE_TTL_MS and the agent's history stays
# intact and queryable. Revocation here can only ever be more restrictive than
# this file, never less.
# OB1_AGENT_CACHE_TTL_MS=60000

# ── Model provider ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Two calls happen on every capture: an embedding (load-bearing — without it a
# thought is invisible to search) and a metadata extraction (enrichment — topics,
# people, type; a failure here degrades gracefully). Both go to one
# OpenAI-compatible endpoint, OB1_LLM_BASE_URL, and the shipped defaults are
# LOCAL: qwen3-embedding:4b at 1024 and qwen2.5:7b, via Ollama, no credential.
#
# Where the server finds Ollama is the one line to choose. Unset, compose.yaml
# points the server at the stack's own `ollama` service (Option A). The code's
# own default, 127.0.0.1:11434, is the right address from a shell on the host
# and the CONTAINER's loopback from inside it, which reaches nothing — so the
# compose file, not the code, names the default here.
#
# Pick ONE of Options A–C below; Option D, further down, gives the chat calls a
# second endpoint beside whichever you chose.

# ── Option A: the stack's own Ollama (the default) ───────────────────────────
# Start with:  podman compose -f deploy/compose.yaml --profile local-models up --build
# Nothing to set: the profile adds the `ollama` service and pulls both models.
# Leave OPENROUTER_API_KEY empty; a local provider needs no credential.
# OB1_LLM_BASE_URL=http://ollama:11434/v1

# ── Option B: an Ollama on the host — macOS, where the container has no GPU ──
# `ollama serve` on its 127.0.0.1 default is enough; the VM reaches the host's
# loopback by this name (podman machine — measured; Docker Desktop:
# host.docker.internal). Skip the profile. SETUP.md, "On macOS, install Ollama
# natively instead".
# OB1_LLM_BASE_URL=http://host.containers.internal:11434/v1

# ── Option C: OpenRouter — the text of every capture leaves the host ─────────
# Uncomment the URL line too, not the key alone: with only a key set the
# server still dials the stack's own Ollama (Option A's fallback) and sends
# the key there — preflight warns, and the first capture fails on a host that
# does not exist without the profile. The models change as a pair with the
# endpoint: a local model name sent to a hosted endpoint 404s on every capture.
# OB1_LLM_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# OB1_EMBEDDING_MODEL=openai/text-embedding-3-small
# OB1_EMBEDDING_DIM=1536   # hosted; unmeasured, see evals/README.md
# OB1_METADATA_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o-mini

# ── Extraction sampling (any endpoint) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Temperature 0, and no reasoning pass on a thinking model (off/false/0 and
# unset mean none; on/true/1 lets it think at its default effort; any other
# word — low, medium, high — is sent as its reasoning_effort; whitespace
# around a value is not part of it).
# OB1_METADATA_TEMPERATURE=0
# OB1_METADATA_REASONING=off

# ── The local models (Options A and B) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Chosen by measurement, not size — see evals/README.md.
# The default. Best measured on real data (0.933 MRR vs embeddinggemma's 0.914)
# and the only local model that embeds a long capture whole. 2560 native, so it
# relies on Matryoshka truncation to fit pgvector's 2000-dimension index limit —
# enabled automatically for known-MRL models, no flag needed.
# OB1_EMBEDDING_MODEL=qwen3-embedding:4b
# OB1_EMBEDDING_DIM=1024
#
# Cheaper alternatives, both ~620 MB against 2.5 GB and ~3x faster:
#   qwen3-embedding:0.6b @ 1024   0.918 MRR
#   embeddinggemma       @ 768    0.916 MRR
# OB1_METADATA_MODEL=qwen2.5:7b

# ── Option D: two providers — embeddings on one, chat on another ─────────────
# The embedding call goes to OB1_LLM_BASE_URL; the chat calls (metadata
# extraction, the chunk blurbs, the supersession judge) go there too unless
# OB1_CHAT_BASE_URL names another endpoint. Local embeddings with a hosted chat
# model keeps the whole text of every capture on the host for the vector and
# sends it out only for tagging; a second local runtime that serves chat only
# (a streaming-MoE engine, an LM Studio beside Ollama) sits next to a local
# embedder the same way. OB1_METADATA_MODEL then names a model the CHAT
# endpoint serves.
#
# A credential belongs to an endpoint: a different chat endpoint gets
# OB1_CHAT_API_KEY and never inherits OB1_LLM_API_KEY or OPENROUTER_API_KEY —
# both belong to the embeddings endpoint, whatever their names suggest — so a
# local chat model beside a hosted embedder is not handed the hosted key, and
# local embeddings with OpenRouter for chat is OB1_CHAT_API_KEY, not
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY. The same base spelled in both knobs is one endpoint and
# shares the key. preflight prints a row for each endpoint and fails a hosted
# one that has no key of its own.
# OB1_CHAT_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
# OB1_CHAT_API_KEY=

# ── The supersession judge's model ───────────────────────────────────────────
# db/consolidate.ts judges pairs of thoughts with OB1_METADATA_MODEL unless this
# names another model the chat endpoint serves. The judge is the harder of the
# two tasks — one 7B extracts tags well and judges every pair at floor
# confidence (SMD-1873) — and this runs it on a stronger model without moving
# every capture's tagging with it. The pass key carries the model, so a change
# starts a fresh pass over the pool; nothing else changes. Unset means the
# metadata model. Under the local-models profile, ollama-pull pulls it too.
# OB1_JUDGE_MODEL=

# ── The typed-decision tier (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────
# A Jev-class model answers a bounded question — "is this true of this text?",
# "which of these options?" — with a calibrated probability in one forward
# pass, no generation (SMD-2050). The Jev spikes (a reranker, a question
# router, extraction gates) call it through server-portable/jev.ts; the server
# itself never does. Off unless OB1_JEV_BASE_URL is set; set, preflight dials
# it on every start and FAILS when it does not answer — so set it only with
# the tier running: `--profile jev` (then http://jev:8020) or `bun
# jev/serve.ts` on the host (then http://127.0.0.1:8020 from a checkout, and
# http://host.containers.internal:8020 from the server container under podman
# machine on macOS; serve.ts binds loopback, which rootless podman on Linux
# does not reach — use the profile there). "Local" is
# declared here too, as for the model provider: without OB1_JEV_LOCAL=1 the
# egress gate treats the tier as remote and, under the default, refuses every
# decision. OB1_JEV_MODEL names the model you expect (verdict-v1.4 today);
# a tier serving another is refused. Unset, whatever the tier serves — every
# answer names its model, revision and weights hash either way.
# OB1_JEV_BASE_URL=http://jev:8020
# OB1_JEV_LOCAL=1          # OB1_JEV_BASE_URL is on this machine or its private network
# OB1_JEV_MODEL=verdict-v1.4

# ── What may leave the box ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every model call carries a thought's full text. Before the call, the egress
# gate decides whether that text may reach an endpoint that is not on this
# box (SMD-1903):
#   deny   (the default)  nothing leaves unless an OB1_EGRESS_ALLOW term matches
#   allow                 everything leaves unless an OB1_EGRESS_DENY term matches
#   off                   no gate — the endpoint you typed is the whole policy
#
# "Local" is DECLARED, never guessed from the address. A loopback URL, the
# host alias (Option B) and the stack's own `ollama` service (Option A) are
# all remote to the gate until the flag says otherwise — so Options A and B
# set OB1_LLM_LOCAL=1, and Option C leaves it unset: the default then refuses
# every capture's embedding until you say, in words, what may leave. A
# refused capture still lands — text and fingerprint, no vector, the reason
# in the reply and on the audit row — and a later re-embed pass fills the
# vector in. preflight prints the mode and, per endpoint, what leaves.
# OB1_LLM_LOCAL=1          # OB1_LLM_BASE_URL is on this machine or its private network
# OB1_CHAT_LOCAL=1         # OB1_CHAT_BASE_URL likewise; a chat endpoint at the same base is the same box — either knob declares it
# OB1_EGRESS_POLICY=deny
#
# Terms are comma-separated unit:value, matched exactly (case-insensitive):
#   actor:<key name>    the access key writing or searching (MCP_ACCESS_KEYS)
#   source:<value>      the thought's metadata.source — the caller's label at a capture ("mcp" when it gave none), the row's at every later pass; a claim, so gate WHO wrote with actor:
#                       (before SMD-1298 every MCP capture was "mcp" here; a `deny source:mcp` written for that is now an `actor:<key>` term)
#   type:<value>        metadata.type — known only for rows already tagged (the passes, a re-embed)
#   topic:<value>       any of metadata.topics — likewise
#   marker:<text>       a literal the text contains, e.g. a #public tag the writer adds
# A term that does not parse fails preflight and the gate fails CLOSED (deny).
# Under deny, the terms say what MAY leave; under allow, what MUST stay.
# OB1_EGRESS_ALLOW=actor:chatgpt,marker:#public
# OB1_EGRESS_DENY=marker:#phi,type:person_note

# ── The embedding width is permanent ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Ask the provider for a NARROWER vector than the model natively emits, via the
# OpenAI `dimensions` parameter. Off unless set to on/1/true/yes.
#
# This is what makes a model wider than pgvector's 2000-dimension HNSW ceiling
# usable: qwen3-embedding:4b emits 2560 and cannot be indexed, but truncated to
# 1024 it scored the best retrieval result measured on real data. Set
# OB1_EMBEDDING_DIM to the width you want and turn this on.
#
# Only safe for models trained for Matryoshka truncation (embeddinggemma,
# qwen3-embedding, openai/text-embedding-3-*). Providers apply the parameter to
# ANY model and return a shortened vector with no warning — on a model not trained
# for it, retrieval quietly gets worse. Measured: roughly twice the MRR loss.
# See evals/README.md.
# OB1_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=on

# Trigram index on thoughts.content. This is what makes search_thoughts_keyword
# fast; without it that tool still returns the right rows, by sequential scan.
#
# ON by default since migration 012 gave the index a caller. It was off before
# that, for the narrow reason that no core query could reach it.
#
# Measured rather than assumed (db/bench-trgm.ts, db/bench-keyword.ts). Below
# ~10,000 rows the planner declines it and it changes nothing; above that a
# 5-row match improves ~350x at 10k and ~1350x at 100k, but a word in 10% of
# rows gets only 8-9x, and a common word or a pattern under three characters
# gets nothing at any size. What it always costs is ~70-95 microseconds on every
# capture and roughly the size of the table again in storage.
#
# So set this to `off` if your brain is small and staying small: you would be
# paying the write cost for a read benefit the planner will not take. Everything
# keeps working.
#
# Read only when migration 011 first applies. Change it later and preflight will
# tell you the setting and the database disagree, with the statement to run —
# which is the state every deployment that applied 011 before 012 is now in.
# OB1_TRGM_INDEX=off

# Migration 023 fingerprints every legacy row in one call, under a table lock
# — about five minutes of waiting writers per 100,000 legacy rows. A brain with
# millions of them sets a batch size for that one run of the migrator, then
# finishes by hand with SELECT backfill_content_fingerprints(<n>) until it
# returns 0 (db/migrations/023_content_fingerprint_backfill.sql, "Cost, and the
# batch path"). Unset is every row. Read only by the migrate service.
# OB1_BACKFILL_LIMIT=10000

# ── Filtered search: the bounds on the HNSW walk (migration 014) ─────────────
# Not environment variables. match_thoughts answers a metadata filter that
# matches at most ~1,000 thoughts exactly, with no index walk; a broader filter
# is applied inside the HNSW scan, which walks until enough rows pass. That walk
# is bounded by two database-level settings that migration 014 seeds once
# (hnsw.max_scan_tuples = 100000, hnsw.scan_mem_multiplier = 8) and never
# overwrites. At the default result count the walk visits about N / 25 tuples,
# so the seeded cap covers tables to ~2.5 million rows; past that, or for very
# large result counts, raise it as the database owner, in one session, and
# restart the server so its pool picks the new values up. The SELECT loads
# pgvector first: a non-superuser may set hnsw.* settings only once the library
# is loaded in the session.
#   SELECT '[1]'::vector;
#   ALTER DATABASE openbrain SET hnsw.max_scan_tuples = 400000;
# A value set in server configuration (ALTER SYSTEM, a parameter group) is
# respected by 014 and never overridden — once it has been RELOADED: 014 reads
# the migrating session's view of the settings, and an ALTER SYSTEM that has not
# been followed by pg_reload_conf() looks unset and is seeded over (a database
# value then outranks the server's; one ALTER DATABASE ... RESET undoes it). So
# reload before migrating. A value set on a single role is not a reason for 014
# to skip seeding the database-level default for other roles.
# Memory: each walk may use work_mem x scan_mem_multiplier (32 MB at defaults),
# two walks per filtered call, per connection — up to ~640 MB across the default
# pool of ten under concurrent broad filters on a large table. Raising work_mem
# raises it too.

# Chunking. A capture longer than the provider's per-request batch (2048 tokens on
# Ollama by default) is embedded only in part, so its tail becomes unsearchable.
# Above a threshold the server splits the capture into overlapping windows and
# embeds each, keeping the whole thing findable. Short thoughts — nearly all of
# them — are untouched and write no extra rows. Unset, both numbers follow the
# embedding model's measured window (db/config.mjs, KNOWN_MODEL_WINDOW): the
# default model windows captures over 4096 estimated tokens at 1200 a window, a
# 2048-token model everything over 1200 at 1200, and preflight prints the rule.
# Setting OB1_CHUNK_TOKENS pins the threshold and the window size to one value.
# OB1_CHUNK_TOKENS=1200
# OB1_CHUNK_OVERLAP=150
#
# Entity extraction (db/extract-entities.ts) windows a long thought the same
# way, at the METADATA model's size, not the embedding model's: each window is
# one call, each call carries an answer budget, and the windows' answers are
# merged before they are written. Unset, the window follows the metadata
# model's served context (db/config.mjs, KNOWN_CHAT_MODEL_WINDOW) and never
# exceeds the size the default model was measured to finish reliably; preflight
# prints the value and the model it came from. Set it for a model the table
# does not list, or a server that serves a smaller context than measured.
# OB1_EXTRACT_CHUNK_TOKENS=1200

# Contextual retrieval: generate a short blurb situating each window in its parent
# document and prepend it before embedding, so a window reading "we settled on
# thirty minutes" carries which system it concerns.
#
# OFF, and measured off rather than assumed off. On 37 queries that name a
# document's subject and ask for a detail living in one window, against the
# default `qwen3-embedding:4b`, it is WORSE than the bare windows this server used
# to store — 0.826 MRR against 0.904, helping one query and hurting eight — and
# further behind what it stores now, which is 0.935. The cause is
# measured too: the blurb moves a window away from its own query, because a
# fixed-size vector has less room for the sentence that actually answers.
#
# Turn it on only after measuring on YOUR corpus with `evals/eval-contextual.ts`.
# The sign depends on the model: the same harness on `embeddinggemma` — 768
# dimensions and a real 2048-token ceiling — reports +0.041 instead. A weaker
# window vector has more to gain from the extra subject signal.
#
# Costs one LLM call per chunk at capture (1.2-2.2s at qwen2.5:7b locally, across
# four runs), on the few captures long enough to chunk. Needs migration 013; preflight refuses to
# start if this is on without it, because the blurbs would be embedded and then
# silently discarded.
# OB1_CHUNK_CONTEXT=on

# The model and OB1_EMBEDDING_DIM must agree, and CANNOT change once you have
# captured anything without re-embedding every row. Choose now, while empty.
#   openai/text-embedding-3-small  1536
#   embeddinggemma                  768   best measured local option
#   bge-m3                         1024   ties it; multilingual
#   nomic-embed-text                768   measurably worse — see evals/
# text-embedding-3-large returns 3072, which exceeds pgvector's HNSW limit of
# 2000 — the column works but no index can be built, so every search is a scan.
# migrate.ts refuses a mismatched pair, and preflight refuses a schema that
# disagrees with the running config.

# Only needed if your provider is not OpenRouter and not local.
# OB1_LLM_API_KEY=

# Seconds a single provider call — an embedding, a blurb, the metadata extraction
# on every capture — may take before it is abandoned; the server and
# db/reembed.ts both read it. Generous on purpose — a whole long
# document on a local model, or a blurb from a large chat model, can take tens of
# seconds — because what it exists for is the call that never returns: a hung
# provider used to park a re-embed worker until the second Ctrl-C. Empty, zero
# or non-numeric mean the default.
# OB1_LLM_TIMEOUT=120

# ── The query log (migration 034) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Off until set to on: one row per search (the query text, its arguments, the
# ids returned), per follow-up fetch/edit/delete of a returned id, and per id a
# write cites as its source — personal data at rest, in the same database, so a
# retrieval change can be replayed against real use (evals/eval-replay.ts) and
# whether callers use what comes back measured (evals/eval-utilization.ts).
# SETUP.md says what it stores; preflight reports it ON or off in the server
# log. prune_query_log() keeps this many days of it.
# OB1_QUERY_LOG=on
# OB1_QUERY_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ── The pipeline tier this server runs as (SMD-1806) ─────────────────────────
# stable | canary | working. Stamped onto every query_log row the server writes,
# so the canary — which replays stable's log — can tell a stable-written row
# from its own. Unset is a plain brain (the tier column is NULL); the three-tier
# stack (deploy/compose.tiers.yaml) sets it per service. Preflight's `tier` check
# reports it, and warns when it disagrees with the tier the database was stamped.
# OB1_TIER=stable

# ── The board in the brain (SMD-1954; `--profile board-sync`) ────────────────
# The `board-sync` service keeps the brain in lockstep with a Linear board:
# every OB1_BOARD_SYNC_INTERVAL seconds it lists the issues of the initiative's
# projects and writes the difference — a new issue captured, a moved or renamed
# one updated in place, nothing twice — as the actor `board-sync`, through the
# same store and embedder a capture uses (db/sync-linear.ts). It needs a Linear
# personal API key (https://linear.app/settings/api; read-only use — it fetches
# issues) and the initiative whose projects are the board, by name or by a
# prefix that names exactly one. What it pulls is your tracker's text, embedded
# by the provider above under the egress policy above. Off unless the profile
# is asked for; the interval must be at least 10 seconds.
# LINEAR_API_KEY=lin_api_...
# OB1_LINEAR_INITIATIVE=Open Brain
# OB1_BOARD_SYNC_INTERVAL=300

# ── What the host can reach ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every port the stack publishes names its address, and the default is loopback:
# only this machine reaches the server. The database and Ollama are not published
# at all unless compose.host-ports.yaml is added as a second -f (for a tool run
# from a checkout — db/reembed.ts, the evals); psql and pg_dump go through
# `compose exec postgres` with the -f files the stack runs with. Unset here
# means 127.0.0.1.
#
# For a remote client — a claude.ai connector, another machine — put a TLS
# proxy or a tunnel (caddy, cloudflared, tailscale serve) in front, and note
# that one running on THIS host dials 127.0.0.1:${SERVER_PORT} itself: the
# loopback default already serves it, and SERVER_BIND stays unset. Set
# SERVER_BIND=0.0.0.0 only when the proxy is on another machine; it puts the
# key on the wire in clear on every request (a header over plain HTTP is as
# visible as `?key=` in the URL), so never bare on a network you do not own.
# The others bind the database superuser and unauthenticated model APIs (Ollama,
# and the typed-decision tier under --profile jev); leave them on loopback.
# SERVER_BIND=127.0.0.1
# POSTGRES_BIND=127.0.0.1
# OLLAMA_BIND=127.0.0.1
# JEV_BIND=127.0.0.1
#
# The three-brain pipeline (compose.tiers.yaml, SMD-1806) publishes one server per
# tier, each on loopback by default. Same rule: leave unset for 127.0.0.1, set a
# _BIND only for a proxy on another machine.
# STABLE_SERVER_BIND=127.0.0.1
# CANARY_SERVER_BIND=127.0.0.1
# WORKING_SERVER_BIND=127.0.0.1

# ── Optional ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPEN_BRAIN_CITATION_BASE_URL=https://your-dashboard.example/thoughts
# OB1_PG_POOL=10
# SERVER_PORT=8000
# The host ports of the database, Ollama and the typed-decision tier, read only
# with compose.host-ports.yaml.
# POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# OLLAMA_PORT=11434
# JEV_PORT=8020
# The three-brain pipeline's per-tier server ports (compose.tiers.yaml, SMD-1806);
# leave OB1_TIER unset for that stack — each server defaults to its own tier.
# STABLE_SERVER_PORT=8010
# CANARY_SERVER_PORT=8011
# WORKING_SERVER_PORT=8012
# /dev/shm for the postgres container (compose `shm_size`). A parallel HNSW
# build keeps its whole graph here, sized by maintenance_work_mem; the 1g
# default covers a rebuild of a few hundred thousand rows, and a bulk load of
# a real corpus wants it at least as large as the maintenance_work_mem the
# rebuild runs with — or max_parallel_maintenance_workers = 0 for a serial
# build in ordinary backend memory. A tmpfs cap, backed only as used.
# POSTGRES_SHM_SIZE=1g
